  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1988-p17.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "If \\(x\\le0,\\) the first equation gives \\(y=10,\\) contradicting the second; hence \\(x>0.\\) If \\(y\\ge0,\\) the second gives \\(x=12,\\) contradicting the first; hence \\(y<0.\\) The equations become \\(2x+y=10\\) and \\(x-2y=12.\\) Thus \\(x=\\frac{32}{5},\\ y=-\\frac{14}{5},\\) and \\(x+y=\\frac{18}{5}.\\)\n\nThus the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Use each equation to rule out one sign possibility",
        "After determining the signs of \\(x\\) and \\(y,\\) solve a linear system"
      ],
